=== L1 What Democracy Means ===
id: 691c70f8-ac5f-4fe8-a644-3a5a955e474e
slug: what-democracy-means
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:35:58.373599+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 23

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Different forms of democracy and the values that underpin democratic society.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">What Is Democracy?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2"><dfn class="term" data-def="A system of government in which power rests with the people, either exercised directly or through elected representatives.">Democracy</dfn> is one of the most important concepts in Citizenship Studies. The word comes from the ancient Greek words <em>demos</em> (people) and <em>kratos</em> (power) &mdash; democracy literally means &ldquo;power of the people.&rdquo; At its heart, democracy means that those who are governed have a say in how they are governed. Not all democracies work in exactly the same way, so it is important to understand the different forms democracy can take.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Direct and Representative Democracy</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">In a <dfn class="term" data-def="A form of democracy in which citizens vote directly on laws and decisions, rather than electing representatives to decide on their behalf.">direct democracy</dfn>, citizens make decisions themselves. Ancient Athens is the most famous example: free male citizens gathered in an assembly and voted on laws directly. Today, referendums are the closest equivalent to direct democracy in the UK. When the UK held a referendum on EU membership in 2016, every eligible voter had an equal say &mdash; the result was 51.9% in favour of leaving and 48.1% in favour of remaining, a factual outcome on which different citizens hold very different views about its significance.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n5">Most modern democracies, including the UK, use <dfn class="term" data-def="A form of democracy in which citizens elect representatives to make decisions on their behalf.">representative democracy</dfn>. Citizens choose MPs, councillors and other officials to represent them and make decisions in their name. The elected representatives are accountable to voters &mdash; if voters are unhappy with their performance, they can be voted out at the next election. This system is practical for large, complex societies where direct votes on every issue would be impossible.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n6" data-revision-tip="Cover this and write from memory: two differences between direct democracy and representative democracy, with one example of each.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Direct democracy involves citizens voting on decisions themselves (e.g. a referendum). Representative democracy involves citizens electing representatives to make decisions on their behalf (e.g. a general election to choose MPs). The UK mainly uses representative democracy but also uses direct democracy through referendums on major constitutional questions.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n7">The Values That Underpin Democracy</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n8">Democracy is not just a voting system &mdash; it rests on a set of core values without which elections alone are not enough to make a society truly democratic. The AQA specification identifies five values that underpin democracy: rights, responsibilities, freedoms, equality, and the <dfn class="term" data-def="The principle that everyone, including governments and officials, is subject to the law and that no one is above it.">rule of law</dfn>.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n9"><strong>Rights</strong> are entitlements that citizens hold, such as the right to free speech, the right to a fair trial, and the right to vote. In the UK, these are protected by the Human Rights Act 1998, which incorporates the European Convention on Human Rights into domestic law. <strong>Responsibilities</strong> are the duties that citizens owe in return &mdash; obeying the law, paying taxes, and respecting the rights of others. Rights and responsibilities are two sides of the same coin: a democracy functions only when citizens exercise both.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n10"><strong>Freedoms</strong> include the freedom to express opinions, to hold a religion, to assemble peacefully, and to access information. These freedoms allow citizens to participate meaningfully in public life. <strong>Equality</strong> means that all citizens are equal before the law and have an equal right to participate &mdash; regardless of race, gender, religion, or class. Historically, this was not always the case in the UK: women only gained equal voting rights with men in 1928, and many other groups faced discrimination that had to be challenged through law and civic action.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Misconception: Is a referendum result always legally binding?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n11">Many students assume a referendum is automatically legally binding because &ldquo;the people have spoken.&rdquo; In UK constitutional law, this is not automatically the case. Referendums in the UK are <dfn class="term" data-def="A vote in which all eligible citizens can vote on a single issue; in the UK, referendum results are advisory unless legislation specifically makes them binding.">advisory</dfn> unless Parliament passes specific legislation requiring the result to be acted upon. Parliament remains sovereign and can, in principle, choose not to act on a referendum result &mdash; though doing so would be politically very difficult. This connects to the concept of the sovereignty of Parliament, which means Parliament is the supreme law-making authority in the UK. The debate about whether referendums should be made legally binding is itself a contested political question, with supporters arguing they better reflect the public will and critics arguing they can bypass parliamentary scrutiny.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n12">The Rule of Law</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n13">The rule of law is especially important to democracy. It means that government and officials must act within the law, that laws apply equally to everyone, and that there are independent courts to enforce them. Without the rule of law, a government could change or ignore laws to suit itself. The UK&rsquo;s judiciary (courts and judges) is independent of the government &mdash; this independence protects citizens from arbitrary use of power. The rule of law also includes the principle of the <dfn class="term" data-def="The legal principle that a person is considered innocent until the prosecution proves their guilt beyond reasonable doubt.">presumption of innocence</dfn> &mdash; a cornerstone of the criminal justice system.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n14" data-revision-tip="Test yourself: list the five values that underpin democracy from memory, then write a one-sentence explanation of each.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The five values underpinning democracy are: <strong>rights</strong> (entitlements of citizens), <strong>responsibilities</strong> (duties citizens owe), <strong>freedoms</strong> (liberties to act and speak), <strong>equality</strong> (equal treatment before the law) and the <strong>rule of law</strong> (no one is above the law). All five must be present for a democracy to be genuine.</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Two viewpoints: Is the UK a fully functioning democracy?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n15">One viewpoint is that the UK is a well-established democracy: it has regular free and fair elections, an independent judiciary, a free press, and legal protections for civil liberties. Citizens can stand for election, join parties, and protest peacefully. The Human Rights Act 1998 gives courts the power to declare legislation incompatible with human rights.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n16">A contrasting viewpoint is that the UK&rsquo;s democracy has significant shortcomings: the unelected House of Lords has legislative power, the First Past the Post electoral system means many votes have little impact on the outcome, and voter turnout has fallen in several recent elections &mdash; raising concerns about democratic engagement. Critics also point to the uncodified constitution as a weakness, arguing it leaves too much power in the hands of the executive without sufficient checks. Both viewpoints contain valid arguments and reflect genuine ongoing debates about democratic reform in the UK.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n17">Why Democracy Matters for Citizens</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n18">Understanding what democracy is &mdash; and what it requires to function &mdash; is the starting point for all of GCSE Citizenship Studies. When you study how Parliament works, how elections are run, how pressure groups operate, or how the law protects rights, you are exploring the practical machinery of democracy. Active citizenship is about citizens taking the values of democracy seriously: participating, holding power to account, and working to make their communities fairer.</p>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n20">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n21">Democracy means power ultimately rests with the people; the UK mainly uses representative democracy (elections) but also direct democracy through referendums.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n22">Five values underpin democracy: rights, responsibilities, freedoms, equality and the rule of law &mdash; all five are needed for democracy to function genuinely.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n23">UK referendums are advisory, not automatically legally binding, because Parliament retains sovereignty; the question of whether the UK&rsquo;s democracy is fully functioning is a legitimate contested debate with arguments on both sides.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n19">For an 8-mark &lsquo;Discuss&rsquo; question about democracy, always present two contrasting viewpoints and use connectives such as &ldquo;however,&rdquo; &ldquo;on the other hand,&rdquo; or &ldquo;in contrast&rdquo; to show you are weighing the argument rather than just listing points. For a 12-mark &lsquo;Evaluate,&rsquo; name at least one real-world example on each side &mdash; for example, the Human Rights Act 1998 as evidence of rights protection, or concerns about low voter turnout as evidence of weaknesses. Reach a clear substantiated judgement in your conclusion: &ldquo;On balance, I argue X because &hellip;&rdquo; A common mistake is writing only about one viewpoint &mdash; this caps your answer in the Developing band. Another frequent error is confusing a referendum (which may be advisory) with a legally binding vote: make sure you distinguish these.</p>

