=== L2 The British Constitution ===
id: a6f1f50e-ed4f-4458-9b99-f3bc1a365e7b
slug: the-british-constitution
status: live tier:both updated:2026-08-15T20:55:45.437872+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 21

--- DESCRIPTION ---
How the UK's uncodified constitution distributes power across its key institutions.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">What Is a Constitution?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Every country needs a set of rules about how power is organised and who can do what. These rules form a <dfn class="term" data-def="The set of fundamental rules, laws and principles that determine how a country is governed, how power is distributed and what rights citizens hold.">constitution</dfn>. In most countries, such as the United States, France and Germany, the constitution is a single written document. The UK is unusual because it does not have a single written constitution document. Instead, the UK has an <dfn class="term" data-def="A constitution that is not contained in a single written document but is made up of Acts of Parliament, court judgements, conventions and other sources.">uncodified constitution</dfn> &mdash; its rules come from several different sources and have developed over centuries.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Sources of the British Constitution</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">The British constitution draws on several sources. The most important are: Acts of Parliament (such as the Human Rights Act 1998, the Parliament Acts 1911 and 1949, and the Devolution Acts of 1998); historical documents such as Magna Carta (1215) and the Bill of Rights (1689); court judgements that establish legal precedents; and <dfn class="term" data-def="Unwritten but accepted practices and traditions that govern how public institutions behave, such as the monarch acting on the advice of the Prime Minister.">constitutional conventions</dfn>. Conventions are not enforceable in court but are considered politically binding &mdash; for example, the convention that the monarch invites the leader of the party with a parliamentary majority to form a government.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n5">This mixture of written laws, historical documents and unwritten conventions means the British constitution is flexible and can be changed by Parliament passing a new Act. Supporters of the uncodified constitution argue this flexibility allows the constitution to adapt to changing circumstances without a lengthy amendment process. Critics argue it leaves too much power in the hands of the government and lacks the clear checks and balances found in codified constitutions. Both viewpoints reflect genuine ongoing debates about constitutional reform in the UK.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n6" data-revision-tip="Cover this and recall: three sources of the British constitution, and one argument for and one argument against an uncodified constitution.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The British constitution is uncodified &mdash; it is not contained in one single document. Its sources include Acts of Parliament (e.g. Human Rights Act 1998), historical documents (e.g. Magna Carta 1215), court judgements and constitutional conventions. The constitution can be changed by Parliament passing new legislation.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n7">The Key Institutions That Share Power</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n8">The British constitution distributes power among several key institutions. Understanding how each fits together is central to understanding how the UK is governed. The three main branches of government are the <dfn class="term" data-def="The branch of government responsible for making laws, in the UK this is Parliament (House of Commons and House of Lords).">legislature</dfn>, the <dfn class="term" data-def="The branch of government responsible for putting laws into practice and running the country, led by the Prime Minister and cabinet.">executive</dfn>, and the <dfn class="term" data-def="The branch of government responsible for interpreting and applying the law through the courts, independent of Parliament and the government.">judiciary</dfn>. The UK also has a constitutional monarchy.</p>

<p data-narration-id="n9">The <strong>legislature</strong> is Parliament &mdash; the House of Commons and the House of Lords. Parliament makes law, scrutinises the government, controls taxation and debates public policy. The House of Commons is elected by the public; the House of Lords is mostly appointed. Parliament is said to be sovereign &mdash; meaning it has the supreme power to make and unmake any law.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n10">The <strong>executive</strong> is the government: the Prime Minister, the Cabinet and government ministers. The Prime Minister leads the government and is appointed by the monarch, by convention, as the leader of the party with a majority in the House of Commons. The Cabinet is a committee of senior ministers who collectively decide policy. The civil service &mdash; permanent, politically neutral officials &mdash; carries out the day-to-day work of government departments, ministries and agencies.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n11">The <strong>judiciary</strong> consists of the courts and judges. The UK&rsquo;s highest court is the Supreme Court, established in 2009, which replaced the Law Lords. Courts interpret law and can declare that government action is unlawful, though courts cannot strike down Acts of Parliament the way constitutional courts can in some other countries. The judiciary&rsquo;s independence from the government is a vital protection for citizens&rsquo; rights.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n12">The <strong>monarchy</strong> plays a largely ceremonial role in the modern UK. The monarch formally opens Parliament, gives royal assent to Bills (formally approving them as law) and invites the Prime Minister to form a government. In practice, the monarch acts on the advice of the Prime Minister and ministers. The political power once held by the monarchy has transferred over centuries to Parliament and the elected government.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>How the constitution is changing: devolution and the Human Rights Act</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n13">The British constitution is not fixed &mdash; it has changed significantly in recent decades. <dfn class="term" data-def="The transfer of legislative and executive powers from Westminster Parliament to regional assemblies or parliaments in Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland.">Devolution</dfn> transferred significant powers to the Scottish Parliament, the Senedd (Welsh Parliament) and the Northern Ireland Assembly following Acts passed in 1998. These bodies have the power to legislate on devolved matters such as health and education in their regions, while Westminster retains reserved powers such as defence and foreign affairs.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n14">The Human Rights Act 1998 incorporated the European Convention on Human Rights into UK law, giving courts the power to declare legislation incompatible with human rights &mdash; though only Parliament can change the legislation itself. The UK&rsquo;s departure from the European Union in January 2020 also changed the constitutional landscape: EU law no longer has supremacy over UK law, and Parliament has reclaimed legislative competence in many areas. These ongoing changes illustrate the flexible, evolving nature of the uncodified constitution.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n15" data-revision-tip="Explain to someone: what is parliamentary sovereignty, and give one example of how it works in practice.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Parliamentary sovereignty means Parliament is the supreme law-making authority in the UK. It can make, amend or repeal any law. No other body &mdash; not even the courts &mdash; can overrule an Act of Parliament. This is a central feature of the British constitution and is linked to the absence of a higher &lsquo;constitutional&rsquo; law that Parliament cannot change.</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Misconception: Does the UK have no written constitution at all?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n16">It is a common misconception that the UK has no written constitution. Many parts of the UK constitution ARE written &mdash; for example, the Human Rights Act 1998, the Parliament Acts of 1911 and 1949, and Magna Carta are all written documents. What makes the UK constitution &lsquo;uncodified&rsquo; is that these rules are not gathered together in a single supreme document as they are in the United States. Instead, the constitution is spread across multiple sources including Acts, conventions, and common law. The distinction to remember is: <em>codified</em> means gathered in one document; <em>uncodified</em> does not mean entirely unwritten.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n18">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n19">The UK has an uncodified constitution &mdash; its rules come from Acts of Parliament, historical documents, court judgements and conventions, not a single written document.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n20">Power is shared between three branches: the legislature (Parliament), the executive (Prime Minister and Cabinet) and the judiciary (courts); the monarchy plays a largely ceremonial constitutional role.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n21">Parliamentary sovereignty means Parliament is supreme and can make or change any law; the constitution is flexible and has changed significantly through devolution, the Human Rights Act 1998 and the UK&rsquo;s departure from the EU.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n17">For an 8-mark &lsquo;Analyse&rsquo; question about the constitution, examiners look for two-sided analysis. A common mistake is describing features of the constitution without evaluating them &mdash; for example, stating that the constitution is flexible without then weighing whether flexibility is a strength or a weakness. Structure your answer: identify and explain the feature (e.g. uncodified constitution), then give an argument in its favour (flexibility, can adapt quickly), then give a counterargument (less clear protection of rights, government can alter it more easily). Name at least one real Act or example (Human Rights Act 1998, Devolution Acts 1998). For 12-mark &lsquo;Evaluate&rsquo; questions, a substantiated conclusion is essential &mdash; phrases like &ldquo;overall, I think the uncodified constitution is a strength because&hellip;&rdquo; earn marks that a simple summary does not.</p>

--- PRACTICE_QUESTIONS ---
