=== L7 Voting Systems in the UK ===
id: 5a37b412-405b-4abc-82bd-11328bdad353
slug: voting-systems-in-the-uk
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:33:28.090136+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 25

--- DESCRIPTION ---
First Past the Post and proportional alternatives, and the strengths and weaknesses of each.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Why Voting Systems Matter</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">The way votes are counted determines who governs. Different voting systems can produce very different outcomes from the same set of votes. The UK uses several voting systems for different elections: <dfn class="term" data-def="A voting system in which the candidate with the most votes in a constituency wins the seat, even if they do not have more than half the total votes cast.">First Past the Post</dfn> (FPTP) for Westminster general elections, and various forms of proportional representation for devolved elections and some local elections. Understanding the strengths and weaknesses of each system is a key citizenship skill.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">First Past the Post (FPTP)</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">Under FPTP, the UK is divided into parliamentary <dfn class="term" data-def="A geographic area whose residents are represented by a single MP in the House of Commons.">constituencies</dfn>. Each constituency elects one MP. Voters mark a cross next to their preferred candidate. The candidate with the most votes wins &mdash; they do not need a majority of votes, just more than anyone else. Westminster general elections use this system for all 650 seats.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n5" data-revision-tip="Cover this and recall: explain in two sentences how FPTP works, naming the key terms constituency and plurality.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Under FPTP, each constituency elects one MP &mdash; the candidate with the most votes wins, regardless of whether they have a majority. This is called a plurality. Westminster general elections take place every five years under this system.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n6">Strengths of FPTP</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n7">Supporters of FPTP highlight several advantages. First, it usually produces a clear result: one party typically wins enough seats to form a majority government with a strong mandate to govern. Strong majority governments can implement their manifesto commitments without being blocked by coalition partners. Second, each MP represents a specific constituency, creating a direct link between voters and their elected representative. Third, the system is simple to understand and quick to count: voters mark one cross, results are declared overnight.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n8">Supporters also argue that FPTP produces stability: governments tend to last a full term rather than collapsing when coalition partners fall out. The 2010&ndash;2015 coalition, though stable by comparative standards, showed the complexity of coalition negotiation that more proportional systems often require.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n9">Weaknesses of FPTP</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n10">Critics of FPTP point to several serious problems. First, the system can produce large majorities from a minority of votes: a party can win a landslide majority in Parliament while winning well under half of the votes cast nationally, because votes for losing candidates in each constituency contribute nothing to the final seat count. Second, many votes are effectively &ldquo;wasted&rdquo; &mdash; in safe seats, the outcome is predictable regardless of the local vote, reducing the incentive to vote. Third, smaller parties that attract votes spread evenly across constituencies may win very few seats even with significant national support, meaning <dfn class="term" data-def="The fairness with which votes translate into parliamentary seats; a system is proportional when parties win seats in rough proportion to their share of the vote.">proportionality</dfn> is low.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Common misconception: evaluating FPTP vs alternatives</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n11">A very common exam error is to list FPTP advantages and disadvantages on one side, and proportional representation advantages and disadvantages on the other side, without comparing the two against each other. Examiners want explicit comparison and a substantiated judgement &mdash; for example: &ldquo;While FPTP produces stable majority governments more reliably than PR, it does so at the cost of proportionality, which means millions of voters are effectively unrepresented. On balance, the gain in stability may or may not outweigh this democratic deficit depending on the criteria used.&rdquo; A side-by-side list without explicit comparison and a conclusion will not reach the top band.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n12">Proportional Representation Systems</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n13">Several forms of proportional representation (PR) are used in UK elections. These systems aim to ensure that parties win seats in rough proportion to their share of the vote, giving smaller parties and minority views better representation.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n14">The <dfn class="term" data-def="A voting system used for Scottish Parliament, Welsh Senedd and London Assembly elections, combining constituency seats (FPTP) with regional list seats to produce a more proportional result.">Additional Member System</dfn> (AMS) is used for elections to the Scottish Parliament and the Welsh Senedd. Voters cast two votes: one for a constituency MP (using FPTP) and one for a regional party list. The regional seats are allocated to correct for the disproportionality of the constituency results, producing an overall result closer to the share of the vote. The Scottish Parliament was designed to make single-party majority government difficult, encouraging coalition or minority government.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n15">The <dfn class="term" data-def="A voting system used for Northern Ireland Assembly elections in which voters rank candidates in order of preference and seats are allocated in multiple rounds until all seats are filled.">Single Transferable Vote</dfn> (STV) is used for Northern Ireland Assembly elections and Scottish local government elections. Voters rank candidates 1, 2, 3, and so on. Votes are transferred according to these preferences until all seats are filled. STV produces highly proportional results and gives voters more choice, but is complex to count and can produce results that are harder for voters to understand immediately.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n16" data-revision-tip="Close this and write from memory: name two proportional systems used in UK elections and state one election each is used for.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The Additional Member System is used for Scottish Parliament and Welsh Senedd elections. The Single Transferable Vote is used for Northern Ireland Assembly elections. Both produce more proportional outcomes than FPTP.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n17">Strengths and Weaknesses of PR</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n18">Supporters of proportional systems argue that they better represent the range of views in society. Smaller parties can win seats, reducing &ldquo;wasted votes&rdquo; and encouraging more people to vote because their vote is more likely to count. Coalitions, which PR tends to produce, can also represent a broader range of voters&rsquo; priorities.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n19">Critics argue that PR produces weak governments: coalition partners may disagree, making it harder to implement a clear programme, and voters may not know which parties will form a government after the election. The relationship between individual voters and a specific representative can also be weakened when large multi-member constituencies replace single-member ones. There is no consensus in the UK about which system is best: this remains a contested political question on which different parties hold different positions.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>The 2011 UK referendum on electoral reform</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n20">In 2011, a UK-wide referendum was held on whether to replace FPTP for Westminster elections with the Alternative Vote (AV) system, in which voters rank candidates in order of preference and the lowest-placed candidate is eliminated in successive rounds until one candidate has more than half the votes. The proposal was rejected: 67.9% voted to keep FPTP and 32.1% voted for AV. The result showed that, at that point, a majority of UK voters preferred to keep the existing system. Supporters of reform argued the question was poorly framed and that AV was not a fully proportional system in any case. The debate about electoral reform continues, with supporters of more proportional systems arguing the 2011 referendum settled only the question of AV, not the broader principle of proportional representation.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n22">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n23">First Past the Post is used for Westminster elections: the candidate with the most votes in each constituency wins the seat, which tends to produce majority governments but can produce disproportionate results.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n24">Proportional systems (AMS for Scottish Parliament and Welsh Senedd; STV for Northern Ireland Assembly) give smaller parties fairer representation but can lead to coalition governments and may weaken the voter-MP link.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n25">Evaluating voting systems means comparing them explicitly &mdash; choosing between stability, proportionality, simplicity and voter-representative links &mdash; rather than just listing features of each.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n21">For an 8-mark Analyse or a 12-mark Evaluate question on voting systems, the most common error is to treat the answer as a list of pros and cons without making an explicit comparison or reaching a clear judgement. Examiners want you to weigh the arguments &mdash; for example: &ldquo;FPTP produces stable majority governments, but at the cost of proportionality. The 2011 AV referendum showed that voters, at least at that time, preferred stability over greater proportionality. However, supporters of PR argue that fairness of representation matters more than ease of government formation.&rdquo; Always name a real-world anchor: name a specific system (AMS, STV), name a specific election it is used for, or reference the 2011 referendum. A conclusion that merely says &ldquo;both systems have advantages and disadvantages&rdquo; without a judgement will not access the top band.</p>

