  "type": "2 marks — State",
  "marks": "1 mark for any one of the following: members of the House of Lords; a person serving a custodial prison sentence; a person who is not registered to vote. One clearly stated answer is sufficient — no further explanation is required."
 },
 {
  "text": "Explain why low voter turnout can be a problem for democracy.",
  "type": "4 marks — Explain",
  "marks": "Up to 4 marks. A strong answer develops a point with cause and consequence. For example: a government elected on low turnout may lack a strong mandate (1 mark) because only a small proportion of the total population voted for it (1 mark), which means it cannot claim to represent the views of most citizens (1 mark), and this weakens its democratic authority when passing laws (1 mark). Answers that state a problem without development score 1–2. Two fully developed points of cause and consequence can score up to 4."
 },
 {
  "text": "Read the extract below and answer the question that follows.\n\nA fictional polling organisation surveyed 1,500 registered voters after the 2024 general election. Their report found: 'Among non-voters, the most common reasons given were disillusionment with all political parties (34%), feeling that their vote would make no difference (28%), and difficulty getting to a polling station on a working day (19%). Only 11% said they were satisfied with the result and had therefore chosen not to vote.'\n\nUsing the extract and your own knowledge, what does this source suggest about ways to increase voter turnout?",
  "type": "4 marks — Source Interpretation",
  "marks": "Up to 4 marks: 1 mark for identifying what the source shows (non-voters give disillusionment and powerlessness as the main reasons for not voting); 1 mark for interpreting the significance of this (making voting easier on a working day addresses only 19% of non-voters, so turnout measures must also tackle engagement); 1 mark for applying own knowledge (linking to proposals such as compulsory voting, online voting, or political education); 1 mark for a substantiated judgement that combines the source and own knowledge (e.g. the source implies that convenience reforms alone would not solve voter apathy since disillusionment is the primary cause — deeper engagement measures are needed)."
 },
 {
  "text": "A student council is debating whether to recommend that the local council pilot online voting for its next parish election. A local civic organisation has said: 'Making it easier to vote will automatically increase democratic participation.'\n\nDiscuss whether making voting easier is the most effective way to increase voter turnout.",
