  "marks": "Mastering: Presents a sustained, balanced discussion with developed points on both sides, uses connective language, names at least one specific real-world example (e.g. Australia's compulsory voting system, the 16+ voting age in Scotland, or online voting trials in Estonia), and reaches a clear overall assessment of which measures are most effective and why.\n\nSecure: Covers both sides with some development, includes at least one named real-world example, and reaches a general conclusion, though the reasoning may not be fully sustained.\n\nDeveloping: Makes relevant points but the discussion is mainly one-sided or lacks real-world examples. May list proposals without weighing their effectiveness.\n\nEmerging: Offers basic points about voting or turnout with little development, no named example, and no comparative judgement about the measures."
 },
 {
  "text": "A local charity has published a report arguing that the voting age for all UK elections should be lowered to 16, in line with the rules for Scottish Parliament and Senedd elections.\n\nEvaluate whether lowering the voting age to 16 is the best way to increase democratic participation among young people.",
  "type": "12 marks — Evaluate",
  "marks": "Mastering: Full two-sided argument with multiple developed points for and against; names at least one specific real-world example per side (e.g. 16+ voting in Scottish Parliament and Senedd elections, or international examples such as Austria's national elections); reaches a substantiated overall judgement that weighs the evidence and explains why one position is stronger.\n\nSecure: Both sides presented with at least one named real-world example; a conclusion is reached, but the evaluative judgement may lack full justification.\n\nDeveloping: Relevant points on both sides but limited development; argument may be mainly one-sided or examples are generic ('in some countries') rather than named.\n\nEmerging: Basic points about voting age with little development; one-sided or descriptive; no real-world named example; no substantiated conclusion.\n\nContent that could be credited — Arguments in favour: Scottish Parliament and Senedd already allow 16-year-olds to vote; 16-year-olds can pay tax, marry and join the armed forces; building civic habits early may create lifelong voters; gives young people a voice on issues that affect them such as education and climate policy. Arguments against: 16-year-olds may lack life experience to make fully informed political decisions; turnout among 18-24 year olds is already lower than older age groups, so extending the age may not improve participation; other factors such as compulsory voting or better civic education may be more effective; changing the law risks party-political manipulation of which age group is more likely to support particular parties."
 }
]

