  "type": "12 marks — Evaluate",
  "marks": "Mastering: Full two-sided argument with multiple developed points for and against each approach; names at least one specific real-world example per side (e.g. the British Medical Association as an insider group regularly consulted on health policy; or the disability-rights campaign leading to the Equality Act 2010 as an example of sustained advocacy using a range of outsider and insider tactics); reaches a substantiated overall judgement that weighs the evidence and explains why one or the other approach, or a combination, is more effective.\n\nSecure: Both sides presented with at least one named real-world example; a conclusion is reached, but the evaluative judgement may lack full justification.\n\nDeveloping: Relevant points on both sides but limited development; argument may be mainly one-sided; examples may be generic ('some pressure groups') rather than named.\n\nEmerging: Basic points about pressure groups or campaigning with little development; no named real-world example; no substantiated conclusion.\n\nContent that could be credited — Arguments for insider group approach: direct access to ministers and civil servants allows policy influence before legislation is drafted; government is more likely to consult groups with recognised expertise; insider groups are perceived as moderate and responsible. Arguments for outsider group / public campaigning: can build mass public support that puts pressure on politicians at election time; does not require existing access or status; historical examples show public campaigns can shift opinion and eventually law; digital tools and social media amplify reach dramatically. Balance: the most effective campaigns often combine both — insider lobbying backed by demonstrated public pressure."
