  "marks": "Arguments in support: Digital tools lower participation barriers (e.g. e-petitions require only internet access, not party membership or wealth); Parliament e-petition website creates a formal accountability route (10,000 = government response, 100,000 = debate); social media enables citizens in rural areas, disabled people, and young voters to engage without attending physical events; direct MP contact by email is easier than writing letters; increased transparency through live-streamed council meetings.\n\nArguments against: The digital divide (elderly, low-income, rural communities with poor connectivity) means digital participation may increase inequality rather than reduce it; slacktivism risk — shallow online actions may substitute for deeper civic involvement; filter bubbles reduce exposure to opposing viewpoints, harming deliberative democracy; security concerns and potential for misinformation in e-voting proposals; government responses to e-petitions are non-binding, limiting real accountability.\n\nMastering: Sustained two-sided evaluation with named examples on each side, clear weighing of evidence, and a substantiated conclusion that goes beyond 'it depends' (e.g. digital democracy has improved reach but not depth of participation, and structural inequalities must be addressed before claiming it is truly more equal).\n\nSecure: Both sides covered with at least one named example per side; reaches a conclusion but it may not be fully substantiated.\n\nDeveloping: Relevant points on both sides but limited development, one-sided, or conclusion is a summary rather than an evaluation.\n\nEmerging: Basic points, little real-world example, descriptive rather than evaluative."
 }
]

