 },
 {
  "text": "Describe two stages of the citizenship action investigation process that come after the initial research stage.",
  "type": "4 marks — Describe",
  "marks": "Up to 4 marks (2 per stage). Stage 1 (planning): students plan what action to take, identify who they will work with, set a timeline and define how they will measure success (2 marks; 1 if identified but not developed). Stage 2 (taking action): students carry out their planned activity (e.g. deliver a petition, run a campaign, attend a meeting) and gather evidence of what they did and any responses (2 marks; 1 if identified but not developed). Stages 5 (assessing impact) and 6 (evaluating the process) are also acceptable if clearly described with development."
 },
 {
  "text": "Read the extract below.\n\nA fictional school's citizenship co-ordinator wrote in a 2024 annual report: 'Last year, our Year 11 students ran four citizenship action projects. Two focused on national issues (mental health funding and environmental policy); two focused on local issues (a pedestrian crossing outside school and improving access to a nearby community garden for wheelchair users). When we asked students to evaluate their impact six weeks after completing the action, the students who had worked on local issues were significantly more confident and specific in their evaluations. One student wrote: \"We got the crossing painted and the council confirmed the new lights would be installed by March.\" By contrast, students who had worked on national issues described their actions in much vaguer terms.'\n\nUsing the extract and your own knowledge, assess what this source tells us about what makes a citizenship action investigation effective.",
  "type": "4 marks — Source Interpretation",
  "marks": "Up to 4 marks: 1 mark for identifying a relevant point from the source (e.g. students working on local, specific issues gave clearer and more confident evaluations); 1 mark for interpreting what this shows (e.g. a focused, locally grounded action is easier to evaluate because the impact is measurable and visible); 1 mark for supporting this with own knowledge (e.g. AQA examiner reports note that specific, focused actions consistently produce stronger evaluations; broad or vague actions make it difficult to show clear impact); 1 mark for a substantiated judgement (e.g. the source suggests that the most effective investigations are those with a clear, specific aim and a measurable outcome, which is consistent with what the specification and examiner guidance recommend)."
 },
 {
  "text": "A student has completed a citizenship action investigation in which they led a campaign asking the school to introduce a weekly food waste collection for local community composting. The campaign was partially successful: the school agreed to a trial for one term.\n\nDiscuss whether this citizenship action can be considered effective.",
  "type": "8 marks — Discuss",
  "marks": "Mastering: Well-developed two-sided discussion, considering what was achieved (a formal trial, demonstrating that citizens can influence institutional decisions through evidence-based action), what was not achieved (a permanent programme), and what factors may have influenced the partial success (limited resources, school priorities, time constraints). Uses connectives and weighs contrasting viewpoints with specific reference to the stages of citizenship action investigation. Reaches a substantiated judgement.\n\nSecure: Both sides covered with at least one developed point per side; at least one reference to specific features of the investigation. Two-sided in places; generally accurate.\n\nDeveloping: Points on both sides but develops unevenly; largely one-sided or without specific reference to the investigation stages.\n\nEmerging: Basic points, descriptive, little or no evaluative thinking."
 },
 {
  "text": "Evaluate the view that citizens taking direct action in their local communities is the most effective form of citizenship action.\n\nIn your answer, consider arguments in support of this view, arguments against it, and reach a substantiated overall judgement.",
  "type": "12 marks — Evaluate",
  "marks": "Arguments in support: local actions are more focused and measurable, making evaluation easier and outcomes more visible; local decision-makers (councillors, headteachers, community organisations) are more accessible than national politicians; students and citizens see direct evidence of change, reinforcing civic confidence; examiner evidence shows that locally focused investigations produce stronger evaluations; local campaigns can be completed within a manageable timeframe.\n\nArguments against: local actions may address symptoms rather than root causes that require national policy change; some citizenship issues (e.g. disability rights, national mental health funding) require national action to achieve lasting impact; national campaigns led by citizens (e.g. the Hillsborough families, disability rights organisations) have achieved legislative change that local action could not; digital tools increasingly allow citizens to influence national policy through e-petitions and online campaigns without needing physical presence.\n\nMastering: Sustained two-sided evaluation with named examples on both sides; weighs evidence carefully; reaches a substantiated conclusion (e.g. local action is most effective for clearly defined, immediate community issues where impact is measurable, but national campaigns have produced lasting legislative change that local action alone cannot achieve; the most effective citizenship actions often start locally and build outwards).\n\nSecure: Both sides with at least one named example per side; reaches a conclusion but may not be fully substantiated.\n\nDeveloping: Relevant points on both sides but limited development, or one-sided, or conclusion is a summary.\n\nEmerging: Basic points, little real-world example, descriptive."
 }
]

